WEBVTT

1
00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:17.420
In today's podcast we will talk about ITHS. With us is Viktor Lakčević.

2
00:00:17.420 --> 00:00:20.560
Viktor, welcome and introduce yourself.

3
00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:27.440
First of all, thank you for the invitation. I am Viktor Lakčević, a third-year student at ITHS and a regular member of the project center.

4
00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:32.640
Okay, Viktor. Tell me, why did you choose ITHS in particular?

5
00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:41.800
Well, I finished primary school in Montenegro, I was born there, and already in primary school I was interested in IT, robotics and programming.

6
00:00:41.800 --> 00:00:45.710
From eighth grade I was looking for which high school that could be.

7
00:00:45.710 --> 00:00:51.440
I couldn't find suitable high schools in my town, so I looked elsewhere.

8
00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:57.840
Maybe I even thought about Europe, but I found that Belgrade is closer and has a great school for IT.

9
00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:05.180
So I enrolled in advance, in eighth grade, went to look at the school and see what the curriculum was like.

10
00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:16.800
They had plenty of material for an IT scholarship, so I got the scholarship approved.

11
00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:21.400
I can say it was the right choice, as it turned out.

12
00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:29.400
And I would advise everyone who doesn't have such an IT school in their town to definitely enroll in an IT school.

13
00:01:29.400 --> 00:01:32.400
Okay, and tell me, which track are you on at ITHS?

14
00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:40.440
At ITHS you mostly choose between the IT track, which is information technology technician, and multimedia.

15
00:01:40.440 --> 00:01:48.400
Already at enrollment they saw my materials, what I had worked on, and said: you are for the IT track, which I confirmed.

16
00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:56.330
The main difference between the two tracks is that IT is more about programming, while multimedia is more about graphics and editing video and images.

17
00:01:56.330 --> 00:02:01.880
On the IT track they have programming, web programming and databases, which multimedia doesn't have.

18
00:02:01.880 --> 00:02:06.400
There it is more image processing, video material processing, filming and so on.

19
00:02:06.400 --> 00:02:12.400
Tell me, are there any other extracurricular activities, and which ones?

20
00:02:12.400 --> 00:02:20.400
Well, besides the rich curriculum at school there is, of course, something for those who want to learn more, as in my case in the first year.

21
00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:27.400
Already in the first year I became a member of the project center, the first since the school's founding to get involved in the first year.

22
00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:38.960
Of course there are extracurricular activities. Not all sections were the same, I myself took the initiative to create new sections as well.

23
00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:49.600
I can say that, in agreement with teachers and the school administration, various sections can be created that there is interest in.

24
00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:54.910
So, besides many sections, I am a member of the project center.

25
00:02:54.910 --> 00:02:58.400
That section has existed since the school was founded.

26
00:02:58.400 --> 00:03:03.730
These are mostly sections for students with more knowledge of IT and robotics.

27
00:03:03.730 --> 00:03:07.940
Usually some projects with electronics and robotics are made,

28
00:03:07.940 --> 00:03:11.030
and those projects are presented at competitions.

29
00:03:11.030 --> 00:03:14.400
We just had a competition during the summer break.

30
00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:18.400
Sorry to interrupt, these are like real little robots, right?

31
00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:18.780
Yes.

32
00:03:18.780 --> 00:03:20.400
And how do they work, how does it go?

33
00:03:20.400 --> 00:03:27.150
Usually we have some idea. Teachers or we ourselves come up with an idea, notice a problem that needs solving,

34
00:03:27.150 --> 00:03:29.210
and see what parts we need.

35
00:03:29.210 --> 00:03:33.400
The school orders the parts needed and we form a team of five or six members.

36
00:03:33.400 --> 00:03:36.720
Of course nobody does the same thing, because we don't all know the same.

37
00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:40.440
Whoever knows programming better does the programming part.

38
00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:43.630
Whoever knows hardware better assembles the robot.

39
00:03:43.630 --> 00:03:46.890
Whoever knows more about marketing, those are the multimedia students.

40
00:03:46.890 --> 00:03:52.400
They make, for example, a website to present it or video material for the school's site.

41
00:03:52.400 --> 00:03:56.790
And when a project is finished, we have competitions. That started this summer.

42
00:03:56.790 --> 00:04:02.400
We mark the birthdays of scientists. This summer it was Nikola Tesla Day, where there were projects.

43
00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:10.900
There I won first place with an air purifier project made in the project center, which got a lot of attention.

44
00:04:10.900 --> 00:04:14.400
It's mostly about the heavy pollution in Belgrade.

45
00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:18.080
Here is a pure example of how an idea for a project comes about.

46
00:04:18.080 --> 00:04:21.630
We just open the window and see that the pollution is heavy.

47
00:04:21.630 --> 00:04:25.640
We see air purifiers that cost two or three hundred euros,

48
00:04:25.640 --> 00:04:28.590
and that is too big a sum for an ordinary citizen

49
00:04:28.590 --> 00:04:32.400
to buy in order to have clean air in their apartment or house.

50
00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:38.180
And there the idea was born to make a budget air purifier, which I built.

51
00:04:38.180 --> 00:04:42.330
It's about 3D printing. The whole casing is 3D printed,

52
00:04:42.330 --> 00:04:46.960
and inside are the same HEPA filters that go into those big purifiers,

53
00:04:46.960 --> 00:04:52.130
only at a better price, because you pay the purchase price and everything is made on the spot,

54
00:04:52.130 --> 00:04:55.400
plus an ordinary fan that filters that air.

55
00:04:55.400 --> 00:04:58.560
And how does it work? On batteries, on electricity?

56
00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:01.400
It runs on electricity, from an ordinary outlet.

57
00:05:01.400 --> 00:05:08.910
That problem existed in Australia in 2020, when there was a big fire, for example.

58
00:05:08.910 --> 00:05:15.660
People had money to buy purifiers, but there was a big shortage.

59
00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:20.890
They mostly just took one filter, put a fan on it

60
00:05:20.890 --> 00:05:26.400
and made it as a DIY thing, at home, air purifiers.

61
00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:31.420
The second project was smart heartbeat monitoring, that is, ECG.

62
00:05:31.420 --> 00:05:35.900
The idea grew out of the problem of someone tracking blood pressure

63
00:05:35.900 --> 00:05:39.400
or anything else and writing those values down on paper.

64
00:05:39.400 --> 00:05:43.060
Day after day, I took a sensor for tracking heartbeat

65
00:05:43.060 --> 00:05:46.580
and connected it to a microcontroller called Arduino.

66
00:05:46.580 --> 00:05:51.160
It is very common in school projects because it was made for schools.

67
00:05:51.160 --> 00:05:55.400
I connected all of that with cables to various electronic components.

68
00:05:55.400 --> 00:06:01.320
The point is that there is a small screen on which the ECG is shown, the measurement, that is, the pulse.

69
00:06:01.320 --> 00:06:04.900
When you click the button it is sent to a live web server

70
00:06:04.900 --> 00:06:10.400
and that information is available to everyone, i.e. the patient and the doctor when he goes for a checkup.

71
00:06:10.400 --> 00:06:16.350
There is an option to link all measurements of heartbeat, ECG or blood pressure

72
00:06:16.350 --> 00:06:18.640
and print an Excel table,

73
00:06:18.640 --> 00:06:21.310
you can see the exact time when it was measured,

74
00:06:21.310 --> 00:06:24.890
what the value was, and a graph where you can

75
00:06:24.890 --> 00:06:28.400
see the increase or decrease of those values.

76
00:06:28.400 --> 00:06:31.160
That is something you came up with, right?

77
00:06:31.160 --> 00:06:36.100
Yes, it was all part of those three projects, and we built a weather station too.

78
00:06:37.180 --> 00:06:44.340
In the project center this is closely connected with the air purifier: a small house with a solar panel on top,

79
00:06:44.340 --> 00:06:48.590
which would stand outdoors, on a tree or a pole,

80
00:06:48.590 --> 00:06:53.250
and collects information about temperature, humidity and air pollution.

81
00:06:53.250 --> 00:06:59.660
It also sends it to a web server and automatically, when the air is polluted, the purifier in our home turns on.

82
00:06:59.660 --> 00:07:08.600
The problem in Belgrade and in the world is misinformation: we may see that the air is clean, while a website says it's polluted, or the other way around.

83
00:07:08.600 --> 00:07:12.710
So we don't have to depend on anyone, we have our own station.

84
00:07:12.710 --> 00:07:22.570
It was an idea for a Hackathon competition in a town in Montenegro, where the town was to be modernized, and that's when I came up with the idea.

85
00:07:22.570 --> 00:07:27.040
I realized it later at ITHS, and back then it was only a sketch,

86
00:07:27.040 --> 00:07:31.930
because the hackathon lasted four hours and you had to come up with some idea.

87
00:07:31.930 --> 00:07:36.120
I presented there with my team and we won first place.

88
00:07:36.120 --> 00:07:42.570
In that town it was planned to have several such weather stations in different parts of town

89
00:07:42.570 --> 00:07:44.910
so the measurements could be compared.

90
00:07:44.910 --> 00:07:50.660
All of that is a kind of digitalization of smart cities, I can say, with those projects.

91
00:07:50.660 --> 00:07:53.660
And do you have any new ideas now?

92
00:07:53.660 --> 00:08:09.090
Well, as I said, in Serbia there are plenty of scientists' days, there was Tesla Day, and soon we have Mihajlo Pupin Days, where there is a literary contest for students who write,

93
00:08:09.090 --> 00:08:11.630
and there is also a best IT idea.

94
00:08:11.630 --> 00:08:18.660
There students mostly present games they make and some websites.

95
00:08:18.660 --> 00:08:26.670
I'm working on projects I made earlier, because it's September now, the summer break has only just ended.

96
00:08:26.670 --> 00:08:31.360
I haven't managed to come up with anything new, honestly, but I hope

97
00:08:31.360 --> 00:08:37.020
there will be new projects during the next year. I already have something in my head.

98
00:08:37.020 --> 00:08:40.660
So we are constantly working on some new projects.

99
00:08:40.660 --> 00:08:45.220
I would add: we also had a start-up competition.

100
00:08:45.220 --> 00:08:51.610
For the best start-up idea an EU fund invested 10,000 euros across three places.

101
00:08:51.610 --> 00:08:57.170
The ideas were developed by ITHS, ITS and the faculty of contemporary arts.

102
00:08:57.170 --> 00:09:01.920
So high-school teams met with university students.

103
00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:05.930
It wasn't easy to break through in that competition

104
00:09:05.930 --> 00:09:11.040
and come up with an idea that could be turned into a product.

105
00:09:11.040 --> 00:09:18.800
Our team from ITHS made it into the top five projects, but unfortunately we didn't win a prize.

106
00:09:18.800 --> 00:09:25.820
More of the students were represented, who had a bit more time and resources

107
00:09:25.820 --> 00:09:31.660
to research an idea, but our project was definitely very noticed.

108
00:09:31.660 --> 00:09:43.200
Okay. As for extracurricular sections, are there maybe some other sections for students with different interests?

109
00:09:43.200 --> 00:09:51.720
Well, this is already, I think, the third year that Lea Moon is organized, which is the Link Educational Model Alliance.

110
00:09:51.720 --> 00:09:56.180
It includes all the Link Education high schools in Belgrade.

111
00:09:56.180 --> 00:10:01.700
This year it is organized by ITHS, together with the International school and Savremena.

112
00:10:01.700 --> 00:10:04.990
It is a simulation of the UN.

113
00:10:04.990 --> 00:10:10.500
It's a kind of diplomacy where public speaking and public appearance are practiced.

114
00:10:10.500 --> 00:10:20.700
Students are encouraged to think about a broader picture, not only in IT but also about the global political topics happening in the world.

115
00:10:20.700 --> 00:10:23.010
It has been held at ITHS for two years now.

116
00:10:23.010 --> 00:10:28.610
We recognized that our school's space is the best and that we have the most rooms for it,

117
00:10:28.610 --> 00:10:33.160
and I can say that all the participants were delighted with the space and the campus.

118
00:10:34.160 --> 00:10:38.700
This year it was a bit bigger than last year, about 160 participants.

119
00:10:38.700 --> 00:10:43.540
I can say they are from all schools, but mostly from international ones,

120
00:10:43.540 --> 00:10:45.770
of course there are state schools as well.

121
00:10:45.770 --> 00:10:50.850
There were several topics as at the real UN, where they have a Security Council,

122
00:10:50.850 --> 00:10:57.920
a General Committee for humanitarian actions, where they get a topic and a country they represent.

123
00:10:57.920 --> 00:11:01.080
And there the expression applies: not "I", but "we".

124
00:11:01.080 --> 00:11:08.040
Because when they speak, they don't represent themselves as persons, they represent a country.

125
00:11:08.040 --> 00:11:14.810
They simulate that every country has several people representing it at the UN,

126
00:11:14.810 --> 00:11:18.420
they get a topic and write their position paper.

127
00:11:18.420 --> 00:11:30.800
That's what it's about: stating your country's position, who your allies are, which countries have a similar policy, with whom you can make a resolution.

128
00:11:31.500 --> 00:11:36.710
It lasts three days, Friday and the weekend. Usually the first day is the opening ceremony.

129
00:11:36.710 --> 00:11:42.800
This year we had the honor to host the ambassadors of Denmark and Sweden, who came to give a speech.

130
00:11:42.800 --> 00:11:46.390
It was an interactive debate on global issues

131
00:11:46.390 --> 00:11:49.980
that are popular in Denmark and Sweden and mostly at the UN.

132
00:11:49.980 --> 00:11:54.520
These are people with great diplomatic experience on missions in Africa.

133
00:11:55.380 --> 00:12:01.630
The Danish ambassador talked precisely about her mission in the Sahel region.

134
00:12:01.630 --> 00:12:04.900
She talked about it right there at Lea Moon.

135
00:12:04.900 --> 00:12:09.660
So she gave her honest opinion about it.

136
00:12:09.660 --> 00:12:20.350
And it's a kind of tradition: at the opening we invite someone from the political, diplomatic world, simply to support it and give some of their influence,

137
00:12:20.350 --> 00:12:25.610
because when participants see that someone like that is coming, they get more interested,

138
00:12:25.610 --> 00:12:30.960
and the lectures or debates that are discussed are very interesting too.

139
00:12:31.760 --> 00:12:39.470
After that they had two days of debates and sessions where topics are discussed, and the last day was the closing ceremony,

140
00:12:39.470 --> 00:12:44.260
and resolutions are written as at the real UN, countries that support the resolution,

141
00:12:44.260 --> 00:12:48.210
how much each country contributes to the budget, how much money is needed

142
00:12:48.210 --> 00:12:50.220
and what the solution to that problem is.

143
00:12:50.420 --> 00:12:54.100
I can say we built an organization in which high-school students,

144
00:12:54.100 --> 00:12:58.320
because mostly high-school students take part, there were a couple of participants from universities,

145
00:12:58.320 --> 00:13:02.000
solve global issues and write real resolutions

146
00:13:02.000 --> 00:13:03.960
that could contribute something one day.

147
00:13:04.740 --> 00:13:08.250
Okay. Tell me, are you satisfied with ITHS?

148
00:13:08.250 --> 00:13:23.340
Well, with everything we've talked about, all within ITHS, I can freely say, viewers can close that question: of course I am, because of so many sections and everything, and the organization of ITHS itself.

149
00:13:24.140 --> 00:13:26.300
And how is it for you now, for example, at school?

150
00:13:27.100 --> 00:13:36.940
Well, now it's the third year, that's the step above. The first two years have general subjects, such as history, geography, biology and so on.

151
00:13:36.940 --> 00:13:42.840
And in the third year we have more professional subjects, web programming, applied technologies.

152
00:13:42.840 --> 00:13:47.560
Yes, professional subjects where we work on more practical projects.

153
00:13:47.560 --> 00:13:51.430
And we no longer even carry books to school because they aren't needed.

154
00:13:51.430 --> 00:13:53.620
So you only have a laptop, a tablet?

155
00:13:53.620 --> 00:13:57.560
Yes, everything is done on the computer, we have digital notebooks.

156
00:13:57.560 --> 00:14:01.860
When we write code, we write it in class and just upload it to the notebook.

157
00:14:01.860 --> 00:14:04.560
We come home and can see it all.

158
00:14:04.560 --> 00:14:06.440
That's really interesting.

159
00:14:06.440 --> 00:14:10.850
We don't even have to wear a uniform to be in class.

160
00:14:10.850 --> 00:14:16.820
Everything is digitalized and that is more interesting to me, I enrolled because of IT.

161
00:14:16.820 --> 00:14:25.560
In the third year there are more professional subjects and we are already starting to work on some real practical projects.

162
00:14:25.560 --> 00:14:31.560
Besides the school competitions, did you have any individual competitions in robotics?

163
00:14:31.560 --> 00:14:35.870
Well, in primary school there are many competitions too, where I made many contacts

164
00:14:35.870 --> 00:14:41.260
which led to me getting an invitation this year to be a member of the Montenegrin national team

165
00:14:41.260 --> 00:14:45.340
for a robotics competition, the robotics olympiad, which was held in Athens.

166
00:14:45.880 --> 00:14:49.220
Last year it was in Singapore, before that in Geneva.

167
00:14:49.220 --> 00:14:53.700
This year we got Athens, where the first Olympic Games took place,

168
00:14:53.700 --> 00:14:58.240
and the opening ceremony was at the stadium where the first Olympic Games were held.

169
00:14:58.240 --> 00:15:05.630
It was a wonderful experience, I got back a few days ago from Athens, an experience everyone should go through.

170
00:15:05.630 --> 00:15:16.140
Our team's policy is that the same members can't go every year, it changes, so unfortunately that was the only competition of that kind for me,

171
00:15:16.140 --> 00:15:21.440
but I understand why they do it, because as many kids as possible really should go through it.

172
00:15:21.440 --> 00:15:25.810
At the competition a robot is built around the summer break.

173
00:15:25.810 --> 00:15:30.780
That is the period when they give their challenge. We get a challenge.

174
00:15:30.780 --> 00:15:37.420
This year the robot had to collect some balls and put them into a basket.

175
00:15:37.420 --> 00:15:41.440
They are like baskets, as when you play basketball.

176
00:15:41.440 --> 00:15:45.640
The robot has three movement systems.

177
00:15:45.640 --> 00:15:50.240
It has a lift onto which the balls are placed.

178
00:15:50.240 --> 00:15:59.440
This year the balls are a bit bigger, so it has to lift them and put them in the basket.

179
00:15:59.440 --> 00:16:02.440
Each country plays 12 matches like that.

180
00:16:02.440 --> 00:16:06.710
There were 180 countries. It's, I say, a world competition.

181
00:16:06.710 --> 00:16:09.720
One alliance plays against another.

182
00:16:09.720 --> 00:16:12.090
One alliance has three teams.

183
00:16:12.090 --> 00:16:16.440
It is randomly generated: three teams, that is, three countries.

184
00:16:16.440 --> 00:16:24.060
Usually, before every match, which is very interesting, a strategy has to be made about who does what in the alliance.

185
00:16:24.060 --> 00:16:28.440
One robot collects balls, another robot puts the balls in the basket.

186
00:16:28.440 --> 00:16:37.970
And the main theme of that competition and the whole First Global Championship organization is friendship and working together.

187
00:16:37.970 --> 00:16:54.650
At the end of each round the robots have to climb onto one ramp, both ours and our opponents', without any blocking or sabotage, all balanced, because if they don't, they don't get the bonus points, which are very important.

188
00:16:54.650 --> 00:16:57.460
This year that is called the Multiplier.

189
00:16:57.460 --> 00:17:08.270
So people from the other teams we play against come and say: okay, we're against you, but let's come up with a tactic so that all of us win.

190
00:17:08.270 --> 00:17:16.440
Nobody plays against anybody, because it doesn't matter which alliance wins, what matters is how many points get collected.

191
00:17:16.440 --> 00:17:22.870
This year my team was in 76th place in the world, the best in the region, which is also a notable place.

192
00:17:22.870 --> 00:17:30.120
Last year we were third in the world, so the difference between third and 76th place is big, because it's a two-digit place,

193
00:17:30.120 --> 00:17:33.440
but we still kept the title of best in the region.

194
00:17:33.440 --> 00:17:40.840
It lasted five days and I can say it was very interesting, because we had stands like at a fair,

195
00:17:40.840 --> 00:17:44.730
where each country presents itself and various souvenirs.

196
00:17:44.730 --> 00:17:52.440
I brought home souvenirs from Costa Rica, for example, from African countries, postcards, key chains,

197
00:17:52.440 --> 00:17:59.770
and participants from other countries come and say let's get to know each other, learn something about your country,

198
00:17:59.770 --> 00:18:04.220
where we give some promotional materials and they give us theirs.

199
00:18:04.220 --> 00:18:07.330
It was a wonderful experience, a bit exhausting,

200
00:18:07.330 --> 00:18:09.440
but a wonderful experience anyway.

201
00:18:09.440 --> 00:18:19.440
Okay. Tell me, finally, would you advise young people to enroll in ITHS?

202
00:18:19.440 --> 00:18:22.900
Well, I can say, if they are interested in IT

203
00:18:22.900 --> 00:18:27.290
and if they value practical work more than traditional learning,

204
00:18:27.290 --> 00:18:31.930
they should enroll in ITHS, there they have the freedom to learn and do what they want,

205
00:18:31.930 --> 00:18:34.440
because it is very flexible in that.

206
00:18:34.440 --> 00:18:36.950
So if they want to work in IT,

207
00:18:36.950 --> 00:18:40.710
if maybe they aren't sure whether it's IT or something else,

208
00:18:40.710 --> 00:18:45.360
as I mentioned, we also have sections that aren't related only to IT.

209
00:18:45.360 --> 00:18:48.460
I think it's an excellent start to schooling

210
00:18:48.460 --> 00:18:52.440
and a good springboard for some other university later.

211
00:18:52.440 --> 00:18:55.480
Okay, thank you very much, we're done.

212
00:18:55.480 --> 00:18:56.440
Thank you.
